        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "[codex] refill 128-worker review capacity",
        "body": "## Why\n\nClawSweeper's checked-in scheduler had been reduced to a 32-worker model. It also kept each scheduled background sweep behind one workflow-wide concurrency lock until the older run finished publishing, which could leave workers idle during a long tail.\n\nThis restores the intended 128-worker fleet without making exact review less responsive.\n\n## What changed\n\nThe central budget is now 128 workers. Its derived defaults become 89 normal-review shards, 44 hot-intake shards, 51 repair workers, and 6 commits per commit-review page. The existing exact-review admission limits remain 20 globally and 16 per target repository; imported cluster repair remains capped at 2.\n\nBroad background workflow runs can overlap, but their planner jobs serialize per target repository. Each planner subtracts live shard jobs from the available budget. A run that has finished its shards and is only publishing counts as zero Codex workers, so the next wave can refill released capacity instead of waiting for the whole workflow to exit. Exact-item planners use run-scoped groups and do not wait behind broad planning.\n\nDashboard defaults, operator docs, limit examples, and regression tests now describe the same model.\n\n## Safety and validation\n\nThe scheduler still reserves 16 workers for interactive work and 8 for matrix expansion, leaving 104 quiet-system background slots. Background lanes continue to yield to active priority work.\n\n`pnpm run check` passes, including build, lint, 558 unit tests, 575 repair tests, both coverage gates, limit drift checks, and formatting. Structured Codex autoreview reported no accepted or actionable findings.\n",
